   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Why not create a sufficiently large, simulated dataset with your function, and pass these data points to geom_ribbon?<br><br>Something like:<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>df </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>ggplot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> aes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  geom_ribbon</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">aes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ymin </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.75</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ymax </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.25</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></div></code></div><br>But using something more meaningful to define your ymin and ymax. Also, is it important that you use such an old version of ggplot?<br><br>Sorry if this is not what you&#39;re after.<br><br>Hefin</div>"
    ],
