   "subject": "RE: filled in curve",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Tahoma;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\">I was trying to use a function directly, to get around creating all the points.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI&#39;m using the old version because my distribution comes with it.  I have just switched to the latest version on one system; the upgrade was a bit of work (get a newer version of R from another source, install the R packages, deal with missing dependencies...). \r\n But I think it&#39;s all working now.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nRoss<br>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\">\r\n<hr>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"Tahoma\"><b>From:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a> [<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a>] on behalf of Hefin Rhys [<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hefin....@bath.edu</a>]<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> Wednesday, December 14, 2016 8:49 AM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> ggplot2<br>\r\n<b>Cc:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">joycer...@gmail.com</a></font></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Tahoma;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\"><div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\"><div style=\"direction:ltr\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"Tahoma\"><br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> Re: filled in curve<br>\r\n</font></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Tahoma;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\"><div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\">\r\n<div></div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">Why not create a sufficiently large, simulated dataset with your function, and pass these data points to geom_ribbon?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSomething like:<br>\r\n<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\">\r\n<code>\r\n<div><span style=\"color:#000\">x\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1000</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\ny </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\ndf </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">\r\n y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\n<br>\r\nggplot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> df</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> aes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\">\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>\r\n  geom_ribbon</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">aes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ymin\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.75</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">\r\n ymax </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1.25</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></div>\r\n</code></div>\r\n<br>\r\nBut using something more meaningful to define your ymin and ymax. Also, is it important that you use such an old version of ggplot?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSorry if this is not what you&#39;re after.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nHefin<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nOn Tuesday, 13 December 2016 19:23:40 UTC, Ross wrote:\r\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Tahoma;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\">Thanks for the pointer--exactly the kind of plot I&#39;m trying to make.  But....<br>\r\ngeom_ribbon works with a set of points; is there anything that will work directly with a function?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAnd the documentation seems a little screwy: <a href=\"http://docs.ggplot2.org/current/geom_ribbon.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://docs.ggplot2.org/current/geom_ribbon.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0nENVftf_mzk2fjStsRuYp\">\r\nhttp://docs.ggplot2.org/current/geom_ribbon.html</a><br>\r\nmentions geom_ribbon and geom_area in the Usage section (Useage?) and title, but the Description section mentions geom_interval.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nRoss<br>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\">\r\n<hr>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"Tahoma\"><b>From:</b>\r\n<a href=\"http://UrlBlockedError.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://UrlBlockedError.aspx&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3yyk1hZ74SintAd_GpQViS\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a> [<a href=\"http://UrlBlockedError.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://UrlBlockedError.aspx&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3yyk1hZ74SintAd_GpQViS\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a>] on behalf of Joyce Robbins [<a href=\"http://UrlBlockedError.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://UrlBlockedError.aspx&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3yyk1hZ74SintAd_GpQViS\">joycer...@gmail.com</a>]<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> Monday, December 12, 2016 6:12 PM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> Boylan, Ross<br>\r\n<b>Cc:</b> ggplot2 ‎[<a href=\"http://UrlBlockedError.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://UrlBlockedError.aspx&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3yyk1hZ74SintAd_GpQViS\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a>]‎<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> Re: filled in curve<br>\r\n</font><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div></div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<div style=\"font-size:small\">geom_ribbon works well -- this should be helpful:</div>\r\n<div style=\"font-size:small\"><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div style=\"font-size:small\"><a href=\"https://gist.github.com/jrnold/6799152\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://gist.github.com/jrnold/6799152&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1VATqKJMvJCaYHzES9bL04\">https://gist.github.com/jrnold/6799152</a><br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n<div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Mon, Dec 12, 2016 at 6:18 PM, Boylan, Ross <span dir=\"ltr\">\r\n&lt;<a href=\"http://UrlBlockedError.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://UrlBlockedError.aspx&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3yyk1hZ74SintAd_GpQViS\">Ross....@ucsf.edu</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\nI would like to plot a density function with fill between the curve and the 0 axis over some interval on the X axis.  Is there a good way to do this?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSo far I have been manually creating a dataset with points along the curve and initial and final points with Y=0, and then using geom_polygon.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nstat_function gives me the curve, but not the fill AFAIK.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI&#39;m using an old version of ggplot2, 0.8.9.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThanks.<br>\r\nRoss Boylan<br>\r\n<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br>\r\n--<br>\r\n--<br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing list.<br>\r\nPlease provide a reproducible example: <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0-s54-htbMiCqpY98Vn4d6\">\r\nhttps://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\nTo post: email <a href=\"http://UrlBlockedError.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://UrlBlockedError.aspx&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3yyk1hZ74SintAd_GpQViS\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\r\nTo unsubscribe: email <a href=\"http://UrlBlockedError.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://UrlBlockedError.aspx&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3yyk1hZ74SintAd_GpQViS\">\r\nggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\r\nMore options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3AvkJgCIdZKDD6Fjovng78\">\r\nhttp://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\n---<br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;ggplot2&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to\r\n<a href=\"http://UrlBlockedError.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://UrlBlockedError.aspx&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3yyk1hZ74SintAd_GpQViS\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw38Jw0F9lsJAlOvVlt3kkNm\">\r\nhttps://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<p></p>\r\n-- <br>\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing list.<br>\r\nPlease provide a reproducible example: <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792367468644000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0-s54-htbMiCqpY98Vn4d6\">\r\nhttps://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a><br>\r\n <br>\r